WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Part 3. You will hear two urban planning students discussing bike-sharing schemes in different cities.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.500
First you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Now that we've done all the research into bike-sharing schemes in cities around the world,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
we need to think about how we're going to organise our report.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Right. I think we should start by talking about the benefits.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
I mean, it's great that so many cities have introduced these schemes,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
where anyone can pick up a bike from dozens of different locations and hire it for a few hours.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
It makes riding a bike very convenient for people.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Yes, but the costs can add up and that puts people on low incomes off in some places.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.000
I suppose so. But if it means more people in general are cycling rather than driving,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
then because they're increasing the amount of physical activity they do, it's good for their health.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
OK, but isn't that of less importance?

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.000
I mean, doesn't the impact of reduced emissions on air pollution have a more significant effect on people's health?

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Certainly in some cities bike-sharing has made a big contribution to that

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
and also helped to cut the number of cars on the road significantly.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Which is the main point.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Exactly. But I'd say it's had less of an impact on noise pollution

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.000
because there are still loads of buses and lorries around.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Right.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Shall we quickly discuss the recommendations we're going to make?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
In order to ensure bike-sharing schemes are successful?

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Yes.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:11.000
OK. Well, while I think it's nice to have really state-of-the-art bikes with things like GPS,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.000
I wouldn't say they're absolutely necessary.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
But some technical things are really important, like a fully functional app

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
so people can make payments and book bikes easily.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Places which haven't invested in that have really struggled.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Good point. Some people say there shouldn't be competing companies offering separate bike-sharing schemes,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.000
but in some really big cities competition's beneficial.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
And anyway, one company might not be able to manage the whole thing.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Right. Deciding how much to invest is a big question.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Cities which have opened loads of new bike lanes,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
at the same time as introducing bike-sharing schemes, have generally been more successful.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.000
But there are examples of successful schemes where this hasn't happened.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
What does matter, though, is having a big publicity campaign.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Definitely. If people don't know how to use the scheme, or don't understand its benefits, they won't use it.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
People need a lot of persuasion to stop using their cars.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Shall we look at some examples now and say what we think is good or bad about them?

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.000
I suppose we should start with Amsterdam, as this was one of the first cities to have a bike-sharing scheme.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Yes. There was already a strong culture of cycling here.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.000
In a way, it's strange that there was such a demand for bike-sharing,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.000
because you'd have thought most people would have used their own bikes.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
And yet it's one of the best-used schemes. Dublin's an interesting example of a success story.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
It must be because the public transport system's quite limited.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Not really. There's no underground, but there are trams and a good bus network.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
I'd say price has a lot to do with it. It's one of the cheapest schemes in Europe to join.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
But the buses are really slow. Anyway, the weather certainly can't be a factor.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.000
No, definitely not. The London scheme's been quite successful.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.000
Yes, it's been a really good thing for the city.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.000
The bikes are popular and the whole system is well-maintained, but it isn't expanding quickly enough.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Basically, not enough's been spent on increasing the number of cycle lanes. Hopefully that'll change.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Yes. Now, what about outside Europe?

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Well, bike-sharing schemes have taken off in places like Buenos Aires.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Hmm. They built a huge network of cycle lanes to support the introduction of the scheme there, didn't they?

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
It attracted huge numbers of cyclists where previously there were hardly any.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.000
An example of good planning.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Absolutely. New York is a good example of how not to introduce a scheme.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.000
When they launched it, it was more than ten times the price of most other schemes.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:48.000
More than it costs to take a taxi. Crazy. I think the organisers lacked vision and ambition there.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:56.000
I think so too. Sydney would be a good example to use. I would have expected it to have grown pretty quickly here.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Yes. I can't quite work out why it hasn't been an instant success like some of the others. It's a shame, really.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.000
I know. OK, so now we've thought about all the...

